<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!-- scott modified the section to allow xi -->
<section xml:id="sec-html-forms"
    class="section"
    xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>HTML Forms</title>
  <p>HTML forms are used to collect user input. They provide a way for users to interact with a web page and submit data to a server (or possibly to be processed in the browser using JavaScript). They are crucial for creating interactive web applications. You've probably filled out many web forms in your life, so you are familiar with their use. Now we need to learn how to build them.</p>
  <p>Forms consist of various input elements such as text fields, drop-down menus, checkboxes, radio buttons, and submit buttons. These elements allow users to enter and submit information. Technically, these are called form controls, but since many of them use the <c>input</c> tag, we will just call them inputs.</p>
  <p>Here is an example of an HTML form that uses some of these elements, though not all, to allow the user to order a pizza:</p>
  <listing>
    <caption>Pizza HTML Form</caption>
    <program interactive="activecode" language="html" include-source="yes">
      <input>
&lt;form method="GET"
         action="/cgi-bin/echo.cgi"&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;&lt;label&gt;Customer name: &lt;input type="text" name="customer"&gt;&lt;/label&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;&lt;label&gt;Telephone: &lt;input type=tel name="phone"&gt;&lt;/label&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;&lt;label&gt;E-mail address: &lt;input type=email name="addr"&gt;&lt;/label&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;&lt;label for="select_size"&gt;Size:&lt;/label&gt;
      &lt;select id="select_size" name="size"&gt;
        &lt;option value=""&gt;choose size&lt;/option&gt;
        &lt;option value="small"&gt;small (10-inch)&lt;/option&gt;
        &lt;option value="medium"&gt;medium (12-inch)&lt;/option&gt;
        &lt;option value="large"&gt;large (14-inch)&lt;/option&gt;
      &lt;/select&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;&lt;label&gt;Preferred delivery time:
      &lt;input name="due" type=time min="11:00" max="21:00" step="900"&gt;
  &lt;/label&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;&lt;label for="textarea_instructions"&gt;Delivery instructions:&lt;/label&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;&lt;textarea id="textarea_instructions"
                name="instructions"
                rows="3" cols="30"&gt;&lt;/textarea&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;&lt;button type="submit"&gt;Submit order&lt;/button&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/form&gt;
      </input>
    </program>
  </listing>
<p>Note that the form is not very pretty, but it is functional.  We will learn how to make it look better in the chapter on CSS. Now let's explore the different types of input elements you can use in forms.</p>
<subsection xml:id="html-forms-anatomy">
  <title>Anatomy of an HTML Form</title>
  <p>Before we dig into the details of forms, let's look at the overall structure. Look carefully at the code above and notice:</p>
  <p>
  <ul>
    <li>There is a <c>form</c> tag that surrounds the entire form. It has some attributes including <c>method</c> and <c>action</c> that we will discuss in more detail later. For now, you should know that these have to do with <em>where</em> the data will be sent and <em>how</em> it will be sent </li>

    <li>
      <p>There are some <c>p</c> tags that mean that the form's input are arranged vertically on the page. You can use pretty much any of the HTML language in creating your form (one exception is that you cannot nest a form with another form), but we've kept this one simple.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li><p>There are some <c>input</c> tags that create empty boxes, such as <c>customer</c> for the user to fill out. </p></li>
    <li><p>Each <c>input</c> element has a <c>name</c> attribute.</p></li>
    <li><p>There is some text next to the <c>input</c> tags, such as <c>"Customer name"</c>.</p></li>
      <li><p>There is a <c>label</c> tag that is new to us; we'll talk about that soon.</p></li>
    <li><p>There is a <c>button</c> at the end of the form that allows the data to be submitted (to wherever the <c>action</c> specifies).</p></li>
      
  </ul>
</p>
</subsection>
<xi:include href="subsec-labels.ptx" />

<subsection><title>WAVE Validation</title>
<p>Because it's so important to ensure that your web forms are accessible, 
  a number of tools have been created to check. This section introduces
  the <url href="https://wave.webaim.org/" visual="https://wave.webaim.org/">
    WAVE Web Accessibility Evaluation Tools</url>. Visit that page and
    you'll see a box labeled (literally) "web page address". That's how you
    can submit a form for validation.</p>
<p>To see it in action, first take a look at this 
  <url href="external/pizza-form.html">Pizza Form</url>, which
  is structured as a standalone web page (rather than being embedded within
  this reading). 
  Copy/paste the URL for the pizza form standalone page into the box on the WAVE page
  and submit (pressing the "enter" key or clicking the arrow will do). 
  In a short time, a report will come back assessing the
  accessibility of the page with the pizza form. </p>
  <p>Now, view this <url href="external/pizza-form-unlabeled.html">Unlabeled Pizza Form</url>. 
    It will probably look identical to you, but we've removed the <c>label</c>
  elements. 
</p><p>  Copy its url into the WAVE tool and get a report. You'll get a lot of complaints about unlabeled inputs. That's what we are trying to avoid, since
  unlabeled forms will be much harder to work with for users of screen readers.
</p><p>As easy as the WAVE tool is to use, we recommend taking a minute or two to install the <url href="https://wave.webaim.org/extension/" visual="https://wave.webaim.org/extension/">WAVE browser plug-in</url>. There are plugins for many popular browsers, it's quick to install, and once you have it, there's a button on your browser that will generate a report for your current page. 
    Furthermore, the browser plugin doesn't require the page to have a public URL, so if you are developing a web site (the purpose of this book), you can test the accessibility of pages that are still under development.</p>
  
</subsection>
<subsection><title>The INPUT tag</title>
  
  <p>The <c>input</c> tag is fairly straightforward, but you can
specify the type of input you are looking for by using the
<c>TYPE</c> attribute.  There are many possible types, and we will not
try to cover all of them; consult <url href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/input" visual="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/input">the MDN page on the input element</url>.</p>
  
  <p>Here are a few of the most common ones:</p>
<p>

<ul>

   <li><c>text</c>: allows the user to enter a word
   or short phrase. This is extremely common in web forms. It gives you a short box (there are ways to control its size) for entering information such as your name, street address, city, state, phone number, and so on.</li>
            
   <li><p><c>password</c>: allows the user to enter in a word or
   phrase, but its value isn't <em>displayed,</em> so no one can look
   over their shoulder and see it.</p></li>
            
    <li><c>email</c>:  similar to the <c>text</c> type, but the browser may check to see that the value looks like an
     email address, which helps to prevent data entry errors.</li>

     <li><c>date</c>: for entering dates.  The browser will often provide a "date picker" widget to make it easy to click on a relevant date, rather than entering it from the keyboard. If the browser doesn't provide a date picker, this type is just like a <c>text</c> input. Ideally, your form should indicate the desired format for the date, such as YYYY-MM-DD.</li>

     <li><c>time</c>: for entering times. As with dates, the browser may provide a widget make it easy to enter times. You can specify what increments you allow (every 5 minutes? every 15 minutes?) and other constraints. As with dates, you should also indicate the desired format, in case the browser falls back on a basic <c>text</c> input.</li>

    </ul></p>

    <p>The <em>purpose</em> of a form is to collect information from the user, and that information is represented as name:value <em>pairs</em>. You can think of it like a little table. For example, the pizza form might be filled out like this.</p>
      <table>
  <title>Sample Data from the Pizza Form</title>
  <tabular halign="left">
    <row header="yes" bottom="minor" >
      <cell>Name</cell>
      <cell>Value</cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell>customer</cell>
      <cell>Hermione Granger</cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell>phone</cell>
      <cell>202-555-1212</cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell>addr</cell>
      <cell>hgranger@hogwarts.ac.uk</cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell>size</cell>
      <cell>small</cell>
    </row>
       <row>
      <cell>due</cell>
      <cell>20:00</cell>
    </row>
       <row>
      <cell>instructions</cell>
      <cell>Please give to Hedwig, not Errol.</cell>
    </row>
  </tabular>

</table>

<p>The consequence of this data representation is that pretty much every input should have a <c>name</c> that is unique within within that form. For example, if you have a form where the user is "signing up" for your web
  application, so you ask them to type their password twice, the html code might look like this.</p>
  <listing>
    <caption>Signup Form</caption>
    <program interactive="activecode" language="html" include-source="yes">
      <input>
&lt;form 
      method="GET"
      action="/cgi-bin/echo.cgi"&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;&lt;label&gt;user name: &lt;input type="text" name="username"&gt;&lt;/label&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;&lt;label&gt;password: &lt;input type="password" name="pwd1"&gt;&lt;/label&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;&lt;label&gt;password again: &lt;input type="password" name="pwd2"&gt;&lt;/label&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;&lt;button&gt;sign up&lt;/button&gt;
  &lt;/form&gt;
      </input></program></listing>
<p>We've said that the purpose of a form is to collect name/value pairs from the user (where you as the 
  form designer have chosen the names of the inputs). Often, you have reason to expect that there is a default
  value for some input, and you want to "seed" the form with that value, both to save time for the vast majority for whom the default is correct, and to help avoid typos. You can do that by supplying a <c>value</c> attribute
  for the input. For example, Hermione might make the following form for her classmates:</p>
  <listing>
    <caption>Form with Default</caption>
    <program interactive="activecode" language="html" include-source="yes">
      <input>
&lt;form method="GET"
      action="/cgi-bin/echo.cgi"&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;&lt;label&gt;Name: &lt;input type=text name="username"&gt;&lt;/label&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;&lt;label&gt;House: &lt;input type=text name="house" value="Gryffindor"&gt;&lt;/label&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;&lt;label&gt;School: &lt;input type=text name="school" value="Hogwarts"&gt;&lt;/label&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;&lt;button type="submit"&gt;Submit&lt;/button&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/form&gt;
      </input></program></listing>
    <p>Hermione's friends in Ravenclaw or even at Durmstrang can just edit the value before submitting.
      (Of course, the downside is that someone might forget to edit the value.)</p>
  <p>An alternative to seeding a particular value is to indicate an example of the kind of value that is wanted.
    This is called <em>placeholder</em> text and given with the <c>placeholder</c> attribute. The placeholder text is only shown by the browser if the input is empty. Note that placeholder text does <em>not</em> substitute for proper labels, but can be helpful in some cases. For example:
  </p>
  <listing>
    <caption>Form with placeholder</caption>
    <program interactive="activecode" language="html" include-source="yes">
      <input>
  &lt;p&gt;&lt;label&gt;nicknames? 
      &lt;input type=text name="nicknames" 
          placeholder="e.g moony or padfoot"&gt;&lt;/label&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
      </input></program></listing>
      </subsection>
